-- title:
--   State-dependent finite selection approximating a hull point
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals.
--
--   1. **Weak limits in hulls.** If a subsequence of $u$ converges weakly to $d$, then $d\in\overline{\mathrm{conv}}\{u_n\}$ (norm closure), since closed convex sets are weakly closed.
--   2. **Adaptive finite selection.** Suppose the weak topology on $K$ is metrizable, $x\in\overline{\mathrm{conv}}\,D$ with $D\subseteq K$, and every $d\in D$ is a weak limit point of $\mathrm{eval}(\mathrm{family}(m))$ for every $m\ge1$. Then for state-dependent thresholds and transitions and every $\tau>0$ there is a finite run $a_0,\dots,a_{N-1}$ with legal transitions, together with convex coefficients $\beta$, such that
--   $$
--   \Bigl\|\sum_i\beta_i\,\mathrm{eval}(a_i)-x\Bigr\|<\tau .
--   $$
--   All candidates are kept in the history, including those with $\beta_i=0$.
